\title{Simulation Extractability and Malleability}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-19}

\p{
    [Knowledge soundness](dhsorens-004J) says a prover who convinces the verifier knows a witness.
    It does not say a prover who has \em{seen} valid proofs cannot produce a new one without knowing
    anything, and that is the property an [on-chain verifier](dhsorens-003P) needs, since every
    proof it has ever accepted is public.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Simulation extractability, weak and strong, and how each differs from knowledge
        soundness — the extractor now works against an adversary with access to simulated proofs.}
        \li{Groth16's malleability, the concrete attacks it has enabled, and why proof uniqueness is
        a separate property from soundness.}
        \li{Whether Fiat–Shamir in the random oracle model gives simulation extractability for free,
        and for which classes of protocol.}
        \li{What a contract actually needs: replay protection by binding the proof to the statement,
        or the cryptographic property. Where nullifiers and domain separation substitute for it.}
        \li{Whether anything in [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) states it, and what a Lean statement would
        have to quantify over.}
    }
}
